+## Submission and Revision Persistence
+
+- [ ] **REC-001**: When an authenticated worker submits a valid visit edit, the record store SHALL retain the original request payload with its visit ID, author ID, and server receipt time.
+- [ ] **REC-002**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with the same authenticated author and an identical complete submission envelope, the record store SHALL return the original acceptance result without creating another revision.
+- [ ] **REC-003**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with a different authenticated author or any different submission-envelope field, the record store SHALL reject the submission with an idempotency-key-reuse error without changing the original revision.
+- [ ] **REC-004**: When edits derived from the same base revision are accepted, the record store SHALL preserve every complete original submission regardless of the later review outcome.
+- [ ] **REC-007**: When the record store accepts a visit submission, it SHALL create a revision containing a server-generated revision UUID, the client-generated visit UUID, the base revision ID, the complete scientific snapshot, the author ID, the idempotency key, and the server receipt time.
+- [ ] **REC-008**: While later submissions or resolutions affect a visit, the record store SHALL keep every existing revision immutable.
+- [ ] **REC-009**: If a visit submission has an invalid envelope, invalid scientific field types, or an invalid plot reference, then the record store SHALL reject it with an invalid-submission error before creating a revision.
+- [ ] **REC-010**: When a valid submission has a null base revision ID for a visit ID that does not exist, the record store SHALL create the visit with the submitted revision as current and return `advanced_current`.
+- [ ] **REC-011**: If a null-base submission names a visit ID that already exists, then the record store SHALL reject the submission with a duplicate-visit-creation error.
+- [ ] **REC-012**: If a non-null base revision ID is unknown or belongs to another visit, then the record store SHALL reject the submission with an error distinguishing those two cases and without changing visit state.
+- [ ] **REC-013**: When the record store rejects a submission whose idempotency key is not already reserved by an accepted submission, it SHALL leave that key unreserved so that a corrected request can reuse it.
+- [ ] **REC-014**: When the record store accepts a submission, it SHALL store an acceptance result containing the revision ID, an outcome of `advanced_current` or `added_to_review`, and the conflict ID when the outcome is `added_to_review`.
+- [ ] **REC-015**: When an accepted submission is retried after its visit state changes, the record store SHALL return the acceptance result stored at the original acceptance time rather than recomputing the outcome.
+
+## Branching and Conflict State
+
+- [ ] **REC-016**: When a settled visit receives a valid submission based on its current revision, the record store SHALL make the new revision current and return `advanced_current`.
+- [ ] **REC-017**: When a settled visit receives a valid submission based on a known non-current revision, the record store SHALL make the visit unresolved by opening a conflict with the previous current revision and new revision as candidates, removing the usable current state, and returning `added_to_review`.
+- [ ] **REC-018**: When an unresolved visit receives an otherwise-valid submission, the record store SHALL add the new revision to the open conflict and return `added_to_review` without advancing current state or resolving the conflict.
+- [ ] **REC-019**: The record store SHALL represent each conflict with a conflict UUID, visit ID, open or resolved state, version, member revision IDs, candidate revision IDs, opening time, and last-change time.
+- [ ] **REC-020**: When a revision joins an open conflict, the record store SHALL retain that revision in the conflict's membership even after a descendant replaces it as a candidate.
+- [ ] **REC-021**: While a conflict is open, the record store SHALL define its candidates as exactly those member revisions that are not ancestors of another member revision.
+- [ ] **REC-022**: When concurrent submissions based on the same current revision of a settled visit contain identical scientific fields, the record store SHALL retain separate revisions and open a conflict for the later submission.
+- [ ] **REC-023**: When a member is added to an open conflict or its candidate set changes, the record store SHALL increment the conflict version.
+- [ ] **REC-042**: When submissions for the same visit are accepted concurrently, the record store SHALL serialize their acceptance per visit so that at most one submission based on a given current revision advances current state.
+- [ ] **REC-043**: The record store SHALL perform submission idempotency checking, accepted-key reservation, revision insertion, acceptance-result storage, and the associated visit-state or conflict-state change in one transaction.
+
+## Resolution
+
+- [ ] **REC-006**: When a coordinator resolves a conflict, the record store SHALL retain a resolution record containing the coordinator ID, resolution time, selected revision ID, reviewed conflict version, and every conflict-member revision ID.
+- [ ] **REC-024**: If an authenticated caller without the coordinator role attempts to resolve a conflict, then the record store SHALL reject the resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-025**: Where a coordinator authored a candidate revision, the record store SHALL permit that coordinator to select the candidate when resolving its conflict.
+- [ ] **REC-026**: When a coordinator resolves an open conflict using its current version and a current candidate revision ID, the record store SHALL make the visit settled with the complete selected candidate as its current revision and no open conflict.
+- [ ] **REC-027**: When a coordinator resolves an open conflict using its current version and a current candidate revision ID, the record store SHALL close the conflict without adding scientific fields or creating a merged revision.
+- [ ] **REC-028**: If a resolution's reviewed conflict version does not match the open conflict's current version, then the record store SHALL reject the resolution with a stale-conflict-version error without changing revision, conflict, or visit state.
+- [ ] **REC-029**: If a resolution selects a known revision that is not a current candidate of the open conflict, then the record store SHALL reject the resolution with a non-candidate-selection error without changing revision, conflict, or visit state.
+- [ ] **REC-030**: If an already-resolved conflict receives any resolution request other than an unchanged retry by the original coordinator, then the record store SHALL reject the request with a conflict-not-open error.
+- [ ] **REC-031**: When the same coordinator repeats a resolution with the same conflict ID, reviewed version, and selected candidate after the original resolution succeeded, the record store SHALL return the original resolution result without creating another resolution record.
+- [ ] **REC-032**: When a settled visit with an earlier conflict resolution receives a valid late submission based on a non-current revision, the record store SHALL open a new conflict without deleting or changing the earlier conflict or resolution history.
+- [ ] **REC-044**: The record store SHALL perform resolution validation, resolution-record creation, conflict closure, and current-revision selection in one transaction.
+
+## Retrieval and History
+
+- [ ] **REC-005**: When a caller retrieves an unresolved visit, the record store SHALL return its visit ID, open conflict ID, open conflict version, and last settled revision ID without a current revision or current scientific snapshot.
+- [ ] **REC-033**: When a caller retrieves a settled visit, the record store SHALL return its visit ID, current revision ID, and complete current scientific snapshot.
+- [ ] **REC-034**: When a caller retrieves an open or resolved conflict, the record store SHALL return the conflict metadata and complete scientific snapshots for the candidate revisions recorded for that conflict, including resolution attribution when the conflict is resolved.
+- [ ] **REC-035**: When a caller lists open conflicts, the record store SHALL return review-queue entries containing each visit ID, conflict ID and version, candidate revision IDs, and opening and last-change times.
+- [ ] **REC-036**: When a caller retrieves visit history, the record store SHALL include every revision, including non-current branches and superseded candidates, with its complete scientific snapshot and original request payload.
+- [ ] **REC-037**: When a caller retrieves visit history, the record store SHALL include revision base links, authorship, server receipt times, conflict membership, and resolution attribution.
+- [ ] **REC-038**: When a caller retrieves visit history, the record store SHALL order revision entries by server receipt time and use revision ID as a deterministic tie-breaker.
+- [ ] **REC-039**: When the record store serves a retrieval operation, it SHALL construct the response from one committed snapshot so that metadata, candidate snapshots, and history entries are mutually consistent.
+- [ ] **REC-040**: If a retrieval or resolution operation names an unknown target visit, conflict, or other resource ID, then the record store SHALL return a distinct not-found error without changing persistent state.
+- [ ] **REC-041**: If a record-store operation fails validation or a concurrency check, then the record store SHALL leave revision, conflict, and visit state unchanged.
